        {
          "published_at": "2026-10-31T14:38:07.871Z",
          "url": "https://x.com/thorstenball/status/2116540225665089755",
          "topic": "Simulation comparing trunk-based, merge-queue, PR, and agentic integration strategies",
          "text": "That's so cool!",
          "quote_tweet": {
            "url": "https://x.com/algo_luca/status/2116520160936833347",
            "published_at": "2026-10-31T13:18:24.067Z",
            "author": {
              "name": "Luca Palmieri",
              "handle": "algo_luca"
            },
            "text": "The trunk-based development debate started by @thorstenball has clearly touched a nerve. So I thought, why don't we look at some numbers?\n\nI built a little simulator with the help of our dear colleague Opus to compare four different integration strategies:\n1. Trunk-based development (@thorstenball's horse)\n2. Merge queue as the only CI gate (what I'm currently using)\n3. PR-based, requiring a fresh base\n4. An agentic merge queue, with built-in conflict resolution (dream option, can't buy it anywhere yet)\n\nYou can play with the simulator here: lpalmieri.com/simulations/co…",
            "links": [
              "https://lpalmieri.com/simulations/commit-integration/"
            ],
